prompt Continue the loop Lindy-first crux-first on the crypto arc; Rye prioritized; approve my own design rounds and make progress before checking in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; a breach rename records a cairn first.
think The ML-DSA ring, encode, sample, keygen, and sign are all GREEN and sent; the pivot doc (20260816-161537) names rung 6/6 as mldsa_verify.rye — the last stone of the ML-DSA ladder and the close of the whole post-quantum arc. By Lindy-first crux-first it is the next durable thing on the road: the public-side check every peer runs to read a post-quantum Kumara name. Read the vendored Zig ml_dsa.zig Signature.verify and PublicKey.fromBytes end to end before writing a line, to fix the exact reconstruction and every decode width.
think Fixed the composition from FIPS 204 Algorithm 8 and Zig's verifyWithContext: decode pk = rho then SimpleBitUnpack(t1, 10); decode sig = c~ then BitUnpack(z, gamma1-1, gamma1) then HintBitUnpack(h); tr = SHAKE256(pk, 64); mu = SHAKE256(tr || 0x00 || 0x00 || M, 64) — the same domain-separator + zero-context-length bytes the signer hashed; c = SampleInBall(c~); A-hat[i][j] = RejNTTPoly(rho, j, i); w'_approx[i] = InvNTT(sum_j A-hat[i][j] dot NTT(z[j]) - c-hat dot NTT(t1[i]*2^d)) = A*z - c*t1*2^d; w1'[i] = UseHint(h[i], w'_approx[i]); c~' = SHAKE256(mu || w1Encode(w1'), ctilde); accept iff ||z||inf < gamma1-beta AND c~' == c~ byte-for-byte.
think Two care points I verified rather than assumed. First, t1*2^d stays a canonical residue: t1 in [0,1023], 1023*8192 = 8380416 = q-1, so no reduction is needed and the assert holds. Second, HintBitUnpack (FIPS 204 Algorithm 21) is where a forged or corrupt signature is caught before any arithmetic — a boundary byte that runs backward or past omega, a position that does not strictly increase within a polynomial, or a nonzero byte past the last boundary all return false. Zig's unpackHint returns null on exactly these, so my reject set matches its InvalidEncoding set.
think A verifier's answer is a boolean, so the doubled oracle is not byte parity but VERDICT parity: agree with a second implementation on accept for a genuine signature and on reject for every tampered one. So the selftest builds a genuine (pk, sig) with Zig's reference keygen + deterministic signer, confirms both our verify and Zig's Signature.verify ACCEPT it, then mutates one byte at a spread of positions (commitment byte 0, a z byte, the last hint boundary, a mid-signature byte, plus a mutated message and a mutated pk) and confirms both REJECT each. A verify that shrugged at any mutation, or disagreed with the stranger, fails. This proves the verify discriminates, not merely that it returns true.
think Two build fixes before GREEN: Zig flagged pk_obj/sig_obj as never-mutated (const), and Signature.fromBytes wants an exact-length array per parameter set, so the tamper buffer is sized [@TypeOf(sig).encoded_length]u8 rather than a fixed 4627. After those, GREEN on the first real run at all three sets.
obs GREEN — our pure-Rye ML-DSA.Verify_internal agrees with Zig std.crypto sign.mldsa Signature.verify on every verdict at ALL THREE parameter sets (ML-DSA-44/65/87) over 5 deterministic seeds and 2 messages each: accepts the exact signature Zig's deterministic signer produced, rejects every single-byte mutation of the commitment, response, hint, message, and public key. The tr/mu hashing, the SampleInBall challenge, the A*z - c*t1*2^d reconstruction, the UseHint high-bit recovery, and the c~' commitment equality all meet Zig's independent implementation at once. The ML-DSA ladder — ring, encode, sample, keygen, sign, verify — is complete end to end, and the whole post-quantum arc (ML-KEM KEM + ML-DSA signature) the pivot doc opened now stands whole.
